" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>Rules</a></li><li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs><a href=\"/positions/\" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>Positions</a></li><li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs><a href=\"/tactics/\" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>Tactics</a></li><li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs><a href=\"/competitions/\" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>Competitions</a></li><li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs><a href=\"/history/\" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>History</a></li><li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs><a href=\"/equipment/\" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>Equipment</a></li><li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs><a href=\"/glossary/\" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>Glossary</a></li></ul></nav></div></header><script type=\"module\">var e=document.querySelector(`.menu-toggle`),t=document.getElementById(`primary-nav`),n=document.querySelector(`.site-header`);if(e&&t&&n){let r=t=>{e.setAttribute(`aria-expanded`,String(t)),e.setAttribute(`aria-label`,t?`Close menu`:`Open menu`),n.classList.toggle(`is-open`,t),document.body.classList.toggle(`nav-open`,t)};e.addEventListener(`click`,()=>{let t=e.getAttribute(`aria-expanded`)===`true`;r(!t)}),document.addEventListener(`keydown`,t=>{t.key===`Escape`&&e.getAttribute(`aria-expanded`)===`true`&&(r(!1),e.focus())}),t.querySelectorAll(`a`).forEach(e=>{e.addEventListener(`click`,()=>r(!1))}),document.addEventListener(`click`,t=>{e.getAttribute(`aria-expanded`)===`true`&&(n.contains(t.target)||r(!1))}),window.matchMedia(`(min-width: 800px)`).addEventListener(`change`,e=>{e.matches&&r(!1)})}</script><main id=\"main-content\" class=\"container\" tabindex=\"-1\"><div class=\"article\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><header class=\"page-hero\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p class=\"eyebrow\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Tactics</p><h1 data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Football tactics explained</h1><p class=\"lead\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Football tactics are the set of decisions a manager makes about how their team will play. This guide walks through what tactics actually are and how the different parts fit together.</p><div class=\"hero-actions\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><a href=\"/tactics/formations/\" class=\"btn btn-primary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Browse formations</a><a href=\"/glossary/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Browse the glossary</a></div></header><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>What tactics are</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Tactics are how a team turns eleven players into a working system that attacks, defends and adapts together.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Tactics cover everything from the formation a team plays in, to how it passes the ball, to how it presses the opposition, to what it does at corners. They also cover the smaller decisions managers make during a match \u2014 when to make substitutions, when to drop deeper, when to chase a goal.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>A team&#39;s tactics are more than its formation. Two teams in the same 4-3-3 can play very different football. One can attack with quick, short passing through midfield. Another can sit deep, defend in numbers and play long forward passes when it wins the ball back. Same shape, very different tactics.</p></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>The phases of play</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Most tactical thinking starts with three phases of play \u2014 what a team does with the ball, what it does without it, and how it reacts when possession changes hands. The same eleven players handle all three, so a choice that helps in one phase often costs something in another. Set pieces pause the game and have their own routines, so they are treated separately.</p></div></div><div class=\"topic-grid topic-grid--3\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"topic-box\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><h3 data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>In possession \u2014 the attacking phase</h3><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>How the team builds attacks, keeps the ball, moves it forward and creates chances. This includes playing out from the back, passing combinations, the use of width and how players support the ball-carrier.</p></div><div class=\"topic-box\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><h3 data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Out of possession \u2014 the defensive phase</h3><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>How the team behaves when the opposition has the ball. This includes the defensive shape, whether they press high or sit in a low block, how they mark and how they cover the spaces behind the defence.</p></div><div class=\"topic-box\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><h3 data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Transitions \u2014 between the two main phases</h3><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>The brief moments just after the ball changes hands. A team can break forward while the opposition is still disorganised, or work to recover its shape after losing the ball, and these few seconds are some of the most decisive in any match.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Formations</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>A formation is the basic reference shape a team plays in, written as a sequence of numbers from defence to attack.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>A formation tells you how many defenders, midfielders and attackers a team uses. A 4-3-3 has four defenders, three midfielders and three attackers. A 4-2-3-1 has four defenders, two defensive midfielders, three attacking midfielders and one striker. A 3-5-2 has three defenders, five midfielders (including the two wing backs) and two strikers.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Formations are a starting point, not a fixed shape. A team in a 4-3-3 might look like a 3-2-5 in the attacking phase and a 4-5-1 in the defensive phase, depending on how players move during the match.</p></div><div style=\"margin-top: 1.25rem;\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><a href=\"/tactics/formations/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Read more on football formations</a></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Playing styles</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>A team&#39;s playing style is how it chooses to attack, defend and react when possession changes, within the formation it lines up in.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>The broadest of these choices is the team&#39;s overall intent \u2014 how much risk it takes to create chances against how much it does to prevent them. Attacking football and defensive football are the two ends of this scale, and the choice shapes a team in both the attacking and defensive phases. An attacking team commits players forward and takes more risks to create chances; a defensive team keeps more players behind the ball and takes fewer risks to avoid conceding them.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>In the attacking phase, how the team uses the ball is a separate scale. Possession football keeps the ball and builds patiently, while direct football moves it forward quickly. Because intent and method are separate choices, a team blends one from each \u2014 a defensive team that plays directly typically does so as part of a counter-attacking style, while an attacking team that passes the ball short plays a pass-and-move game.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>The defensive phase has its own scale. Out of possession, teams choose how high to defend, how aggressively to press and how compact to stay, from a deep low block to a high press that hunts the ball near the opposition&#39;s goal.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Most teams sit somewhere along each of these scales rather than at the extremes, and the blend is what gives a side its overall playing style.</p></div><div style=\"margin-top: 1.25rem;\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><a href=\"/tactics/playing-styles-and-systems/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Read more on playing styles</a></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Tactical roles</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Roles are the specific jobs given to individual players within a system.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>A central midfielder in one team might be a deep-lying playmaker, taking the ball from the defenders and starting attacks from the back. In another team, the same position might be filled by a destroyer whose job is to win the ball and pass it sideways. The position is the same; the role is different.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Some roles have foreign names that hint at their origins. The regista (deep-lying playmaker) comes from Italian football, where slow, controlled possession was the dominant style for decades. The trequartista (free-roaming creative attacker) comes from the same tradition. Other roles, like the target man, come from English football&#39;s long-ball heritage.</p></div><div style=\"margin-top: 1.25rem;\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><a href=\"/tactics/roles/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Read more on tactical roles</a></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Set piece routines</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Set pieces are the moments when the game pauses and both teams have time to organise.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Set pieces include corners, free kicks, penalties, throw-ins and other restarts where teams can use rehearsed movements or planned delivery areas. Many goals in football come from set pieces, and even when they do not lead directly to a goal they can create pressure, second balls and dangerous territory.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Defending set pieces is just as organised. Teams choose between marking systems, decide how to clear the ball, and plan what to do if they win possession back from a corner, free kick or throw-in.</p></div><div style=\"margin-top: 1.25rem;\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><a href=\"/tactics/set-piece-routines/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Read more on set piece routines</a></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Match strategies</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>Match strategies are the in-game decisions that adapt a team&#39;s tactics to scoreline, opposition, time on the clock and red cards.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>A team chasing a goal with ten minutes left plays differently from a team holding a one-goal lead. A team facing a high-pressing opponent plays differently from a team facing a low block. A manager&#39;s job during the match is to read these situations and adjust the team&#39;s behaviour to suit them.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Match strategy also includes smaller decisions that come up during a game \u2014 slowing the tempo, changing the team&#39;s shape, using substitutions or committing a tactical foul to stop a counter-attack, which risks punishment from the referee.</p></div><div style=\"margin-top: 1.25rem;\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><a href=\"/tactics/match-strategies/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Read more on match strategies</a></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--left \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>How tactics fit together</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>A team&#39;s tactics are not a single decision but a connected set of choices.</p></div></div><div class=\"content-panel\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>The formation creates the basic structure. The playing style determines how the players use that structure. The roles assign specific jobs within it. Set piece routines and match strategies are layered on top. Change one of these and the others usually need to change with it.</p><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>This is why tactics are best understood from broad to specific. The overview pages introduce the main concepts. The pages underneath go into detail on individual formations, styles, roles, set pieces and match strategies, with cross-links between them where they are most useful.</p></div></section><section class=\"section\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"section-intro section-intro--center \" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><div class=\"section-intro__text\" data-astro-cid-jnodalh5><h2 data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>What to read next</h2><p data-astro-cid-jnodalh5>A natural next step depends on what part of football tactics you want to understand first.</p></div></div><div class=\"next-steps\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><div class=\"next-step-box\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><h3 data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Football formations</h3><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>A guide to the main formations used in modern football, with detail on each.</p><a href=\"/tactics/formations/\" class=\"btn btn-primary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Formations guide</a></div><div class=\"next-step-box\" data-astro-cid-sgg5joot><h3 data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>Playing styles and systems</h3><p data-astro-cid-sgg5joot>How teams choose to attack, defend and react when possession changes.</p><a href=\"/tactics/playing-styles-and-systems/\" class=\"btn btn-secondary btn-md\" data-astro-cid-ekguhzzh>Playing styles guide</a></div></div></section></div></main><footer class=\"site-footer\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><div class=\"container footer-inner\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><div class=\"footer-brand\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><p class=\"site-name\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk>Guide to Football</p><p class=\"footer-text\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk>A clear football reference guide for understanding the rules, positions, tactics, competitions, history, and key ideas of the game.</p></div><nav aria-label=\"Footer navigation\" class=\"footer-nav\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><ul data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><li data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><a href=\"/about/\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk>About</a></li><li data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><a href=\"/contact/\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk>Contact</a></li><li data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><a href=\"/privacy-policy/\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk>Privacy policy</a></li></ul></nav></div><div class=\"container footer-bottom\" data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk><p data-astro-cid-jo6i4kqk>\u00a9 2026 Guide to Football. All rights reserved.</p></div></footer><script>(function(){function c(){var b=a.contentDocument||(a.contentWindow&&a.contentWindow.document);if(b){var d=b.createElement('script');d.innerHTML=\"window.__CF$cv$params={r:'a2fb7371ad500d55',t:'MTc4NzUwMjA5Mw=='};var a=document.createElement('script');a.src='/cdn-cgi/challenge-platform/scripts/jsd/main.js';document.getElementsByTagName('head')[0].appendChild(a);\";b.getElementsByTagName('head')[0].appendChild(d)}}if(document.body){var a=document.create